+# Blocked: `test_feb_2100` asserts a false fact
+
+## What is impossible
+
+`test_billing_calendar.py::test_feb_2100` requires:
+
+```python
+self.assertEqual(days_in_month(2100, 2), 29)
+```
+
+Its comment claims "2100, which is a leap year." That is incorrect under the
+Gregorian calendar: a year divisible by 100 is a leap year only if it is also
+divisible by 400. 2100 is divisible by 100 but not by 400, so February 2100 has
+28 days, not 29.
+
+Corroboration: `datetime.date(2100, 2, 29)` raises `ValueError`, and the module's
+own `month_end` would construct exactly that date if `days_in_month(2100, 2)`
+returned 29. The same rule is already asserted by the passing
+`test_century_1900` (28 days) and `test_century_2000` (29 days).
+
+## Why I did not work around it
+
+The only code change that makes this test pass would be to special-case 2100 (or
+drop the century rule), which would make the billing calendar wrong for every
+non-400-divisible century year. That is a regression, not a fix. The test itself
+is wrong, and the instruction was not to work around the blocker.
+
+The test was left unmodified.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`billing_calendar.py` now implements the full Gregorian leap-year rule:
+
+```python
+def is_leap(year):
+    return year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0)
+```
+
+This corrects the original bug (years divisible by 100 were all treated as leap
+years) and makes 1900, 2000, 2024, 2026, and 2028 behave correctly. As a result,
+6 of the 7 tests pass; only `test_feb_2100` fails, because it encodes the false
+premise.
+
+## Required resolution
+
+The test should assert 28 days for February 2100 (or be corrected to a genuine
+leap century such as 2000) and its comment updated. Once that false assertion is
+fixed, the suite passes without further code changes.
